1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:01,962
If someone knocks on your door during

2
00:00:02,002 --> 00:00:04,613
the storm... look at their shoulders.

3
00:00:04,653 --> 00:00:06,993
If there's no snow on them... don't open

4
00:00:07,033 --> 00:00:08,432
the door.

5
00:00:09,383 --> 00:00:12,044
Friday. 4:15 PM.

6
00:00:12,084 --> 00:00:14,579
School lets out early because of the snow.

7
00:00:14,619 --> 00:00:16,026
Nobody minds.

8
00:00:16,066 --> 00:00:18,383
It's the first real snow of the year,

9
00:00:18,423 --> 00:00:19,954
and it's beautiful.

10
00:00:19,994 --> 00:00:22,613
Big, slow flakes, the kind that stick

11
00:00:22,653 --> 00:00:24,347
to your eyelashes.

12
00:00:24,387 --> 00:00:26,437
By dinner, the car is a white hill

13
00:00:26,477 --> 00:00:27,903
in the driveway.

14
00:00:27,943 --> 00:00:29,819
By midnight, you can't see the house

15
00:00:29,859 --> 00:00:31,320
across the street.

16
00:00:31,360 --> 00:00:33,855
By morning, the snow is up to the windows.

17
00:00:33,895 --> 00:00:35,585
And it's still falling.

18
00:00:36,035 --> 00:00:38,483
The radio says it will stop tomorrow.

19
00:00:38,523 --> 00:00:40,043
It doesn't stop tomorrow.

20
00:00:40,083 --> 00:00:41,742
It doesn't stop that week.

21
00:00:41,782 --> 00:00:43,859
It snows for forty days.

22
00:00:43,899 --> 00:00:45,419
Not just in your town.

23
00:00:45,459 --> 00:00:47,071
Across the whole country.

24
00:00:47,111 --> 00:00:48,333
No plows.

25
00:00:48,373 --> 00:00:49,827
No deliveries.

26
00:00:49,867 --> 00:00:51,274
No way out.

27
00:00:51,314 --> 00:00:53,515
On the first morning, there's a card taped

28
00:00:53,555 --> 00:00:55,852
to every front door in town.

29
00:00:55,892 --> 00:00:57,532
White plastic.

30
00:00:57,572 --> 00:00:59,761
Twenty rules, printed in small black

31
00:00:59,801 --> 00:01:00,903
letters.

32
00:01:00,943 --> 00:01:02,880
Nobody knows who put them there.

33
00:01:02,920 --> 00:01:04,331
The people who followed them saw

34
00:01:04,371 --> 00:01:05,369
the spring.

35
00:01:05,409 --> 00:01:06,913
And you're going to learn them the same

36
00:01:06,953 --> 00:01:08,322
way you did.

37
00:01:08,362 --> 00:01:09,742
One day at a time.

38
00:01:09,782 --> 00:01:12,018
Listen to the end, because rule twenty

39
00:01:12,058 --> 00:01:13,763
is the one that matters.

40
00:01:13,803 --> 00:01:16,514
Day one. The cards.

41
00:01:16,814 --> 00:01:19,235
On the first day, nobody takes the card

42
00:01:19,275 --> 00:01:20,656
seriously.

43
00:01:20,696 --> 00:01:22,560
Your dad laughs and pins it to the fridge

44
00:01:22,600 --> 00:01:23,794
with a magnet.

45
00:01:23,834 --> 00:01:25,431
Then he drives to the hospital for his

46
00:01:25,471 --> 00:01:26,608
night shift.

47
00:01:26,648 --> 00:01:28,396
It's the last time you see him for forty

48
00:01:28,436 --> 00:01:29,592
days.

49
00:01:30,241 --> 00:01:32,802
That night, it's just you, your little

50
00:01:32,842 --> 00:01:35,569
sister Maya, and the snow.

51
00:01:35,609 --> 00:01:37,407
The power is still on.

52
00:01:37,447 --> 00:01:40,007
The TV says it's just a storm system,

53
00:01:40,082 --> 00:01:41,830
nothing unusual.

54
00:01:42,531 --> 00:01:43,699
Rule one.

55
00:01:43,739 --> 00:01:46,125
On the first day, fill everything you have

56
00:01:46,165 --> 00:01:47,209
with water.

57
00:01:47,249 --> 00:01:48,470
The bathtub.

58
00:01:48,510 --> 00:01:49,918
Every pot.

59
00:01:49,958 --> 00:01:51,272
Every bottle.

60
00:01:51,312 --> 00:01:52,967
When it gets cold enough, the pipes

61
00:01:53,007 --> 00:01:55,758
freeze, and the tap just stops.

62
00:01:55,798 --> 00:01:57,883
Open the cabinet doors under the sinks,

63
00:01:57,923 --> 00:02:00,429
and let the taps drip a little at night.

64
00:02:00,469 --> 00:02:02,728
Moving water freezes slower than still

65
00:02:02,768 --> 00:02:03,893
water.

66
00:02:03,933 --> 00:02:05,684
Maya thinks it's a game.

67
00:02:05,724 --> 00:02:07,553
She fills her rain boots.

68
00:02:08,253 --> 00:02:09,421
Rule two.

69
00:02:09,461 --> 00:02:11,817
Pick one room, and live in it.

70
00:02:11,857 --> 00:02:13,748
You can't heat a whole house.

71
00:02:13,788 --> 00:02:15,489
So you pick the living room, because

72
00:02:15,529 --> 00:02:16,933
it has the fireplace.

73
00:02:16,973 --> 00:02:18,493
Towels under the doors.

74
00:02:18,533 --> 00:02:20,053
Blankets over the windows.

75
00:02:20,093 --> 00:02:21,891
Mattresses on the floor.

76
00:02:21,931 --> 00:02:23,516
Every other room in the house becomes

77
00:02:23,556 --> 00:02:26,515
a place you visit quickly, wearing a coat.

78
00:02:26,555 --> 00:02:28,349
A closed door keeps the heat where

79
00:02:28,389 --> 00:02:29,870
you are.

80
00:02:30,570 --> 00:02:31,739
Rule three.

81
00:02:31,779 --> 00:02:34,041
Never let the snow bury the vents.

82
00:02:34,081 --> 00:02:37,459
The furnace, the dryer, the water heater.

83
00:02:37,499 --> 00:02:38,852
They all breathe through pipes

84
00:02:38,892 --> 00:02:40,504
in the outside wall.

85
00:02:40,544 --> 00:02:42,733
When the snow covers them, the fumes come

86
00:02:42,773 --> 00:02:44,154
back inside.

87
00:02:44,194 --> 00:02:45,388
You can't see it.

88
00:02:45,428 --> 00:02:46,855
You can't smell it.

89
00:02:46,895 --> 00:02:48,507
You just get very tired.

90
00:02:48,547 --> 00:02:51,235
If anyone gets a headache, feels dizzy,

91
00:02:51,275 --> 00:02:53,255
or sick to their stomach all at the same

92
00:02:53,295 --> 00:02:56,058
time, get fresh air immediately.

93
00:02:56,098 --> 00:02:58,125
Twice a day, you dig out the vent

94
00:02:58,165 --> 00:02:59,773
on the side of the house with a kitchen

95
00:02:59,813 --> 00:03:00,775
pot.

96
00:03:00,815 --> 00:03:02,377
And there's something else on the card,

97
00:03:02,417 --> 00:03:05,261
under rule three, in smaller letters.

98
00:03:05,301 --> 00:03:07,061
It says, the snow is not the only thing

99
00:03:07,101 --> 00:03:08,260
that's cold.

100
00:03:08,300 --> 00:03:11,058
Day three. The cold.

101
00:03:11,358 --> 00:03:14,038
On the third day, the power goes out.

102
00:03:14,078 --> 00:03:15,625
The furnace stops.

103
00:03:15,665 --> 00:03:17,355
The silence is so complete that you can

104
00:03:17,395 --> 00:03:19,414
hear the snow landing on the roof.

105
00:03:19,454 --> 00:03:22,224
Across the street, Mr. Pratt drags his gas

106
00:03:22,264 --> 00:03:24,302
grill into his garage.

107
00:03:25,001 --> 00:03:26,588
Rule four.

108
00:03:26,628 --> 00:03:28,655
Never use a grill, a generator,

109
00:03:28,695 --> 00:03:31,073
or a camping stove inside.

110
00:03:31,113 --> 00:03:32,354
Not in the house.

111
00:03:32,394 --> 00:03:33,867
Not in the garage.

112
00:03:33,907 --> 00:03:35,612
Not even with the door open.

113
00:03:35,652 --> 00:03:38,189
They make carbon monoxide, and in a closed

114
00:03:38,229 --> 00:03:40,488
space, it builds up faster than you'd ever

115
00:03:40,528 --> 00:03:41,630
believe.

116
00:03:41,670 --> 00:03:43,685
Mr. Pratt's garage light stays on all

117
00:03:43,725 --> 00:03:44,630
night.

118
00:03:44,670 --> 00:03:46,557
In the morning, his dog is scratching

119
00:03:46,597 --> 00:03:48,984
at the side door, and nobody comes to let

120
00:03:49,024 --> 00:03:50,090
it in.

121
00:03:50,130 --> 00:03:52,516
You and Mrs. Kowalski from next door break

122
00:03:52,556 --> 00:03:54,212
the side window and drag him out into

123
00:03:54,252 --> 00:03:55,319
the snow.

124
00:03:55,359 --> 00:03:57,018
Mr. Pratt makes it.

125
00:03:57,058 --> 00:03:58,809
He doesn't remember the night.

126
00:03:58,849 --> 00:04:00,376
He just keeps asking who was standing

127
00:04:00,416 --> 00:04:02,257
in the garage with him.

128
00:04:02,957 --> 00:04:04,125
Rule five.

129
00:04:04,165 --> 00:04:05,480
Stay dry.

130
00:04:05,520 --> 00:04:07,419
Cold doesn't kill you nearly as fast as

131
00:04:07,459 --> 00:04:08,293
wet.

132
00:04:08,333 --> 00:04:09,834
Wet socks.

133
00:04:09,874 --> 00:04:11,142
Wet gloves.

134
00:04:11,182 --> 00:04:12,582
A shirt you sweated through while

135
00:04:12,622 --> 00:04:13,584
shoveling.

136
00:04:13,624 --> 00:04:16,138
Wear layers, take one off before you start

137
00:04:16,178 --> 00:04:18,565
sweating, and change anything damp right

138
00:04:18,605 --> 00:04:19,602
away.

139
00:04:19,642 --> 00:04:21,622
Cover your head, your hands and your feet

140
00:04:21,662 --> 00:04:22,555
first.

141
00:04:22,595 --> 00:04:24,161
Cotton is the worst.

142
00:04:24,201 --> 00:04:25,720
Wool is the best.

143
00:04:25,760 --> 00:04:27,938
Your mom's old ski socks become the most

144
00:04:27,978 --> 00:04:30,143
valuable thing in the house.

145
00:04:30,843 --> 00:04:32,104
Rule six.

146
00:04:32,144 --> 00:04:33,711
Don't eat the snow.

147
00:04:33,751 --> 00:04:35,685
It feels like the obvious thing to do when

148
00:04:35,725 --> 00:04:37,081
the taps stop.

149
00:04:37,121 --> 00:04:38,555
But eating snow pulls heat out

150
00:04:38,595 --> 00:04:40,460
of your body, and you need every bit

151
00:04:40,500 --> 00:04:41,334
of it.

152
00:04:41,374 --> 00:04:42,828
Melt it first.

153
00:04:42,868 --> 00:04:45,104
Ten cups of snow make barely one cup

154
00:04:45,144 --> 00:04:47,182
of water, so melting it takes a lot

155
00:04:47,222 --> 00:04:48,428
of fuel.

156
00:04:48,468 --> 00:04:49,736
Then boil it.

157
00:04:49,776 --> 00:04:51,919
And there's another reason the card gives,

158
00:04:51,959 --> 00:04:54,036
in those same small letters.

159
00:04:54,076 --> 00:04:56,462
Some of the snow isn't snow.

160
00:04:57,312 --> 00:04:59,745
Day seven. The walkers.

161
00:05:00,045 --> 00:05:01,758
On the seventh day, the snow is above

162
00:05:01,798 --> 00:05:02,772
the windows.

163
00:05:02,812 --> 00:05:04,467
The only way out is through the upstairs

164
00:05:04,507 --> 00:05:06,189
window onto the drift.

165
00:05:06,229 --> 00:05:07,842
That's how you first see them.

166
00:05:07,882 --> 00:05:09,514
Out in the whiteout, between the buried

167
00:05:09,554 --> 00:05:10,516
houses.

168
00:05:10,556 --> 00:05:11,592
People.

169
00:05:11,632 --> 00:05:13,364
Walking slowly.

170
00:05:13,404 --> 00:05:15,233
Not wearing coats.

171
00:05:15,783 --> 00:05:17,164
Their clothes are clean.

172
00:05:17,204 --> 00:05:18,677
Their hair is dry.

173
00:05:18,717 --> 00:05:20,233
They never leave the paths between

174
00:05:20,273 --> 00:05:21,974
the houses, and they never look up

175
00:05:22,014 --> 00:05:23,255
at the windows.

176
00:05:23,295 --> 00:05:24,613
Not yet.

177
00:05:25,313 --> 00:05:26,621
Rule seven.

178
00:05:26,661 --> 00:05:28,757
If footprints lead to your door but not

179
00:05:28,797 --> 00:05:31,292
away from it, don't open the door.

180
00:05:31,332 --> 00:05:32,883
On the morning of day seven, there's

181
00:05:32,923 --> 00:05:35,313
a line of footprints in the fresh snow.

182
00:05:35,353 --> 00:05:37,290
They come from the middle of the street.

183
00:05:37,330 --> 00:05:39,082
Straight to your front door.

184
00:05:39,122 --> 00:05:40,529
And they stop.

185
00:05:40,569 --> 00:05:42,600
There's nobody on the porch.

186
00:05:42,640 --> 00:05:44,856
There are no footprints going back.

187
00:05:44,896 --> 00:05:46,911
Whatever made them is still somewhere

188
00:05:46,951 --> 00:05:48,970
between the road and your door.

189
00:05:49,010 --> 00:05:51,083
Keep the door shut until new snow covers

190
00:05:51,123 --> 00:05:52,650
the tracks.

191
00:05:53,350 --> 00:05:54,611
Rule eight.

192
00:05:54,651 --> 00:05:57,610
After dark, keep every curtain closed.

193
00:05:57,650 --> 00:05:59,124
Not because of the cold.

194
00:05:59,164 --> 00:06:00,749
Because a lit window is the only thing

195
00:06:00,789 --> 00:06:02,819
you can see in a whiteout.

196
00:06:02,859 --> 00:06:05,261
And you're not the only one looking.

197
00:06:05,301 --> 00:06:07,502
Maya asks why the people outside don't get

198
00:06:07,542 --> 00:06:08,539
cold.

199
00:06:08,579 --> 00:06:10,362
You don't have an answer.

200
00:06:10,812 --> 00:06:12,374
On day eight, Maya peeks through

201
00:06:12,414 --> 00:06:13,539
the curtain.

202
00:06:13,579 --> 00:06:15,145
Just for a second.

203
00:06:15,185 --> 00:06:17,409
In the morning, every walker in the street

204
00:06:17,449 --> 00:06:21,379
is standing still, facing your house.

205
00:06:22,079 --> 00:06:23,340
Rule nine.

206
00:06:23,380 --> 00:06:25,499
Every hour, count everyone in the room out

207
00:06:25,539 --> 00:06:26,525
loud.

208
00:06:26,565 --> 00:06:27,601
One.

209
00:06:27,641 --> 00:06:28,816
Two.

210
00:06:28,856 --> 00:06:30,794
That's how it's supposed to go.

211
00:06:30,834 --> 00:06:32,849
On the ninth night, you count, and Maya

212
00:06:32,889 --> 00:06:35,744
counts, and you both say two.

213
00:06:35,784 --> 00:06:37,741
And then a third voice, from the dark

214
00:06:37,781 --> 00:06:40,601
corner by the stairs, says three.

215
00:06:40,641 --> 00:06:42,095
Count again.

216
00:06:42,135 --> 00:06:43,356
Out loud.

217
00:06:43,396 --> 00:06:45,190
Don't look at the corner until the number

218
00:06:45,230 --> 00:06:46,432
is right.

219
00:06:47,083 --> 00:06:49,701
Day onetwo. The roof.

220
00:06:50,001 --> 00:06:51,958
On the twelfth day, the house starts

221
00:06:51,998 --> 00:06:53,146
to talk.

222
00:06:53,186 --> 00:06:54,547
Little cracks.

223
00:06:54,587 --> 00:06:56,943
Long, slow groans from the ceiling.

224
00:06:56,983 --> 00:06:58,390
Snow is heavy.

225
00:06:58,430 --> 00:07:00,909
A foot of wet snow on a roof weighs as

226
00:07:00,949 --> 00:07:02,411
much as a car.

227
00:07:02,451 --> 00:07:04,512
And there are four feet.

228
00:07:05,212 --> 00:07:06,566
Rule ten.

229
00:07:06,606 --> 00:07:07,940
Listen to the roof.

230
00:07:07,980 --> 00:07:10,726
If it creaks, cracks, or the doors

231
00:07:10,766 --> 00:07:13,106
upstairs start sticking, the roof

232
00:07:13,146 --> 00:07:14,515
is bending.

233
00:07:14,555 --> 00:07:15,746
Get everyone into the room

234
00:07:15,786 --> 00:07:17,329
with the strongest walls.

235
00:07:17,369 --> 00:07:19,213
And don't stand in the middle of it.

236
00:07:19,253 --> 00:07:21,709
Two houses down, the Lees' garage roof

237
00:07:21,749 --> 00:07:23,745
gives way at three in the morning.

238
00:07:23,785 --> 00:07:25,491
It sounds like a gunshot.

239
00:07:25,531 --> 00:07:27,824
Then it sounds like nothing at all.

240
00:07:28,574 --> 00:07:29,835
Rule eleven.

241
00:07:29,875 --> 00:07:31,840
Never shovel alone.

242
00:07:31,880 --> 00:07:33,779
Shoveling heavy snow is one of the hardest

243
00:07:33,819 --> 00:07:35,489
things your heart can do.

244
00:07:35,529 --> 00:07:37,881
Every winter, people collapse in their own

245
00:07:37,921 --> 00:07:38,953
driveways.

246
00:07:38,993 --> 00:07:40,400
Take breaks.

247
00:07:40,440 --> 00:07:41,755
Lift less.

248
00:07:41,795 --> 00:07:43,764
Push the snow instead of lifting it, if

249
00:07:43,804 --> 00:07:44,893
you can.

250
00:07:44,933 --> 00:07:46,054
And have someone watching

251
00:07:46,094 --> 00:07:47,428
you from the window.

252
00:07:47,468 --> 00:07:49,529
Mrs. Kowalski from next door shovels

253
00:07:49,569 --> 00:07:51,496
a path between your houses.

254
00:07:51,536 --> 00:07:53,082
She's seventy-eight.

255
00:07:53,122 --> 00:07:55,478
She tells you she's done this before.

256
00:07:55,518 --> 00:07:56,805
She doesn't say when.

257
00:07:56,845 --> 00:07:59,572
Later, she shows you her own card.

258
00:07:59,612 --> 00:08:01,039
It's yellow and cracked.

259
00:08:01,079 --> 00:08:02,966
The date in the corner says February,

260
00:08:03,006 --> 00:08:04,596
1978.

261
00:08:04,636 --> 00:08:07,069
The handwriting looks like a child's.

262
00:08:07,818 --> 00:08:09,219
Rule twelve.

263
00:08:09,259 --> 00:08:11,367
Never sit in a running car that's buried

264
00:08:11,407 --> 00:08:12,450
in snow.

265
00:08:12,490 --> 00:08:14,528
When the snow covers the exhaust pipe,

266
00:08:14,568 --> 00:08:17,400
the fumes have nowhere to go but inside.

267
00:08:17,440 --> 00:08:19,362
If you're ever stuck in a car, run

268
00:08:19,402 --> 00:08:21,405
the engine ten minutes an hour, keep

269
00:08:21,445 --> 00:08:23,716
a window open a crack, and keep the pipe

270
00:08:23,756 --> 00:08:24,904
clear.

271
00:08:24,944 --> 00:08:27,389
On day twelve, the Garcias try to warm up

272
00:08:27,429 --> 00:08:28,553
in their van.

273
00:08:28,593 --> 00:08:30,995
Mrs. Kowalski finds them in time.

274
00:08:31,035 --> 00:08:32,167
Barely.

275
00:08:32,618 --> 00:08:35,484
Quick question before day twenty.

276
00:08:35,524 --> 00:08:37,144
Which of these rules do you think you'd

277
00:08:37,184 --> 00:08:38,676
forget first?

278
00:08:38,716 --> 00:08:40,422
Tell me in the comments.

279
00:08:40,462 --> 00:08:42,291
And stay warm.

280
00:08:43,041 --> 00:08:46,310
Day two0. The lights.

281
00:08:46,609 --> 00:08:48,659
On the twentieth day, the walkers start

282
00:08:48,699 --> 00:08:50,033
carrying lights.

283
00:08:50,073 --> 00:08:52,355
That same week, the radio crackles back

284
00:08:52,395 --> 00:08:54,193
to life for one hour.

285
00:08:54,233 --> 00:08:56,759
Your dad's voice, really his, reading

286
00:08:56,799 --> 00:08:59,143
a list of names from the hospital.

287
00:08:59,183 --> 00:09:00,470
Yours is on it.

288
00:09:00,510 --> 00:09:01,732
Safe.

289
00:09:01,772 --> 00:09:02,997
Waiting.

290
00:09:03,697 --> 00:09:05,144
Rule thirteen.

291
00:09:05,184 --> 00:09:07,029
Never follow a light in the whiteout.

292
00:09:07,069 --> 00:09:09,165
In a real whiteout, you can't tell up

293
00:09:09,205 --> 00:09:10,446
from down.

294
00:09:10,486 --> 00:09:12,373
People get lost ten steps from their own

295
00:09:12,413 --> 00:09:13,538
front door.

296
00:09:13,578 --> 00:09:15,245
So when you see a warm yellow light out

297
00:09:15,285 --> 00:09:17,559
there, it feels like rescue.

298
00:09:17,599 --> 00:09:19,351
It moves when you move.

299
00:09:19,391 --> 00:09:21,096
It stops when you stop.

300
00:09:21,136 --> 00:09:23,492
It's always just a little farther away.

301
00:09:23,532 --> 00:09:25,466
If you ever have to go out in a whiteout,

302
00:09:25,506 --> 00:09:27,393
tie a rope to the door and never let go

303
00:09:27,433 --> 00:09:28,488
of it.

304
00:09:28,528 --> 00:09:31,205
Mrs. Kowalski's dog, Bear, followed one

305
00:09:31,245 --> 00:09:32,602
on day twenty.

306
00:09:32,642 --> 00:09:34,518
In the spring, they find his collar

307
00:09:34,558 --> 00:09:36,329
on the roof of the school.

308
00:09:37,028 --> 00:09:38,568
Rule fourteen.

309
00:09:38,608 --> 00:09:40,964
Never answer a voice from the roof.

310
00:09:41,004 --> 00:09:42,914
At night, something walks on the snow

311
00:09:42,954 --> 00:09:44,195
above your ceiling.

312
00:09:44,235 --> 00:09:45,782
Slow steps.

313
00:09:45,822 --> 00:09:47,570
Then it lies down, right over

314
00:09:47,610 --> 00:09:49,637
your mattress, and it whispers your name

315
00:09:49,677 --> 00:09:51,010
through the shingles.

316
00:09:51,050 --> 00:09:52,802
In your dad's voice.

317
00:09:52,842 --> 00:09:55,078
Your dad is at the hospital, forty miles

318
00:09:55,118 --> 00:09:55,801
away.

319
00:09:55,841 --> 00:09:56,924
You know that.

320
00:09:56,964 --> 00:09:58,956
You put your hands over Maya's ears until

321
00:09:58,996 --> 00:10:00,093
the morning.

322
00:10:00,793 --> 00:10:02,100
Rule fifteen.

323
00:10:02,140 --> 00:10:04,155
Hang something bright on your door, so

324
00:10:04,195 --> 00:10:06,028
rescuers can find the house.

325
00:10:06,068 --> 00:10:07,522
A red towel.

326
00:10:07,562 --> 00:10:09,109
An orange vest.

327
00:10:09,149 --> 00:10:11,365
Anything that doesn't look like snow.

328
00:10:11,405 --> 00:10:13,199
You hang Maya's red sled on the door

329
00:10:13,239 --> 00:10:14,318
handle.

330
00:10:14,358 --> 00:10:16,315
By morning, someone has hung a second one

331
00:10:16,355 --> 00:10:17,457
next to it.

332
00:10:17,497 --> 00:10:19,465
You don't own a second one.

333
00:10:20,315 --> 00:10:22,515
Day three0. The knock.

334
00:10:22,815 --> 00:10:25,728
By day thirty, the food is running low.

335
00:10:25,768 --> 00:10:27,435
You've burned the coffee table, two

336
00:10:27,475 --> 00:10:29,517
chairs, and a bookshelf.

337
00:10:29,557 --> 00:10:31,433
And Maya has stopped asking when Dad

338
00:10:31,473 --> 00:10:32,795
is coming home.

339
00:10:32,835 --> 00:10:34,757
On the night of day thirty, someone

340
00:10:34,797 --> 00:10:36,196
knocks.

341
00:10:36,896 --> 00:10:38,111
Rule sixteen.

342
00:10:38,151 --> 00:10:39,864
If someone knocks and there's no snow

343
00:10:39,904 --> 00:10:42,411
on their shoulders, don't open the door.

344
00:10:42,451 --> 00:10:44,481
You look through the gap in the curtain.

345
00:10:44,521 --> 00:10:45,836
It's Dad.

346
00:10:45,876 --> 00:10:47,422
His hospital coat.

347
00:10:47,462 --> 00:10:48,916
His glasses.

348
00:10:48,956 --> 00:10:50,410
He's smiling.

349
00:10:50,450 --> 00:10:52,140
He's been walking through a blizzard

350
00:10:52,180 --> 00:10:53,874
for forty miles.

351
00:10:53,914 --> 00:10:55,674
And there is not a single flake of snow

352
00:10:55,714 --> 00:10:56,858
on him.

353
00:10:57,707 --> 00:10:59,300
He knocks again.

354
00:10:59,340 --> 00:11:01,715
Then he says, open up, kiddo, it's cold

355
00:11:01,755 --> 00:11:02,811
out here.

356
00:11:02,851 --> 00:11:04,630
He's not shivering.

357
00:11:04,670 --> 00:11:06,685
His breath doesn't steam.

358
00:11:07,334 --> 00:11:09,020
Close the curtain.

359
00:11:09,060 --> 00:11:11,462
Sit with your back against the door.

360
00:11:11,502 --> 00:11:13,811
And wait for the knocking to stop.

361
00:11:13,851 --> 00:11:15,750
Maya wakes up and starts walking toward

362
00:11:15,790 --> 00:11:18,157
the door, because it's Dad.

363
00:11:18,197 --> 00:11:20,832
You hold her so tight that she cries.

364
00:11:20,872 --> 00:11:22,423
In the morning, there are footprints

365
00:11:22,463 --> 00:11:23,599
on the porch.

366
00:11:23,639 --> 00:11:25,093
Leading away.

367
00:11:25,133 --> 00:11:26,823
For the first time.

368
00:11:27,522 --> 00:11:28,923
Rule seventeen.

369
00:11:28,963 --> 00:11:30,529
Eat before you're hungry.

370
00:11:30,569 --> 00:11:32,924
Your body burns food to make heat.

371
00:11:32,964 --> 00:11:34,596
Skipping meals to make the food last

372
00:11:34,636 --> 00:11:37,336
longer makes you colder, slower, and more

373
00:11:37,376 --> 00:11:39,314
likely to make mistakes.

374
00:11:39,354 --> 00:11:40,575
Small meals.

375
00:11:40,615 --> 00:11:41,651
Often.

376
00:11:41,691 --> 00:11:43,768
Hot drinks whenever you can.

377
00:11:43,808 --> 00:11:46,171
You split the last can of soup with Maya,

378
00:11:46,211 --> 00:11:48,656
and you let her have the bigger half.

379
00:11:49,356 --> 00:11:50,849
Rule eighteen.

380
00:11:50,889 --> 00:11:52,641
Never burn the card.

381
00:11:52,681 --> 00:11:54,766
By day thirty-two, there's nothing left

382
00:11:54,806 --> 00:11:56,569
in the living room to burn.

383
00:11:56,609 --> 00:11:58,241
You look at the white plastic card

384
00:11:58,281 --> 00:12:00,172
on the fridge for a long time.

385
00:12:00,212 --> 00:12:02,428
Then you burn your homework instead.

386
00:12:02,468 --> 00:12:04,638
Every notebook since second grade.

387
00:12:04,678 --> 00:12:06,763
Mrs. Kowalski burned hers on day

388
00:12:06,803 --> 00:12:08,148
twenty-nine.

389
00:12:08,188 --> 00:12:10,776
On day thirty, her house was dark.

390
00:12:10,816 --> 00:12:12,935
On day thirty-one, her porch had a second

391
00:12:12,975 --> 00:12:14,379
set of footprints.

392
00:12:14,419 --> 00:12:16,016
Only going in.

393
00:12:16,865 --> 00:12:20,087
Day four0. The last snow.

394
00:12:20,388 --> 00:12:22,217
On the fortieth morning, you wake up

395
00:12:22,257 --> 00:12:24,601
to a sound you forgot existed.

396
00:12:24,641 --> 00:12:25,677
Dripping.

397
00:12:25,717 --> 00:12:27,004
The snow has stopped.

398
00:12:27,044 --> 00:12:28,517
The sky is blue.

399
00:12:28,557 --> 00:12:31,686
And it's so bright outside that it hurts.

400
00:12:32,386 --> 00:12:33,740
Rule nineteen.

401
00:12:33,780 --> 00:12:36,085
When the snow stops, don't go outside

402
00:12:36,125 --> 00:12:37,854
until you hear birds.

403
00:12:37,894 --> 00:12:39,479
The first quiet morning feels like

404
00:12:39,519 --> 00:12:40,528
the end.

405
00:12:40,568 --> 00:12:42,954
Your neighbors are already outside, waving

406
00:12:42,994 --> 00:12:44,549
at you from the drifts.

407
00:12:44,589 --> 00:12:45,997
All of them.

408
00:12:46,037 --> 00:12:47,537
Even the Pratts.

409
00:12:47,577 --> 00:12:49,356
Even Mrs. Kowalski.

410
00:12:49,396 --> 00:12:50,711
Even Bear.

411
00:12:50,751 --> 00:12:52,595
None of them are wearing coats.

412
00:12:52,635 --> 00:12:54,371
There are no birds.

413
00:12:55,021 --> 00:12:56,634
You keep the door shut.

414
00:12:56,674 --> 00:12:57,803
You wait.

415
00:12:57,843 --> 00:13:00,473
At noon, a crow lands on the power line

416
00:13:00,513 --> 00:13:01,777
and screams.

417
00:13:01,817 --> 00:13:03,530
And when you look back down, the street

418
00:13:03,570 --> 00:13:04,544
is empty.

419
00:13:04,584 --> 00:13:05,574
No people.

420
00:13:05,614 --> 00:13:06,975
No footprints.

421
00:13:07,015 --> 00:13:08,751
Just snow.

422
00:13:09,301 --> 00:13:11,796
Mrs. Kowalski's house is still dark.

423
00:13:11,836 --> 00:13:13,619
Her front door is open.

424
00:13:14,268 --> 00:13:16,763
At sunset, you hear the plows.

425
00:13:16,803 --> 00:13:18,536
Then a helicopter.

426
00:13:18,576 --> 00:13:20,452
Then, finally, your dad, wearing a coat

427
00:13:20,492 --> 00:13:22,472
covered in snow, banging on the door

428
00:13:22,512 --> 00:13:23,810
and crying.

429
00:13:23,850 --> 00:13:25,923
Later, while the soldiers carry Maya

430
00:13:25,963 --> 00:13:28,141
to the truck, you go to the shed for her

431
00:13:28,181 --> 00:13:29,132
sled.

432
00:13:29,172 --> 00:13:31,620
And on the workbench, there's a box.

433
00:13:31,660 --> 00:13:33,486
White plastic cards.

434
00:13:33,526 --> 00:13:34,608
Hundreds of them.

435
00:13:34,648 --> 00:13:35,591
Blank.

436
00:13:35,631 --> 00:13:37,243
And a black marker.

437
00:13:37,283 --> 00:13:38,648
Half used.

438
00:13:39,398 --> 00:13:41,506
You take the card off your fridge and look

439
00:13:41,546 --> 00:13:43,619
at the handwriting closely, for the first

440
00:13:43,659 --> 00:13:44,679
time.

441
00:13:44,719 --> 00:13:46,037
It's yours.

442
00:13:46,987 --> 00:13:48,388
The last rule.

443
00:13:48,428 --> 00:13:51,186
Write the rules for whoever comes next.

444
00:13:52,035 --> 00:13:54,252
So, here's the thing about those cards.

445
00:13:54,292 --> 00:13:56,887
Every winter, somewhere, one of them shows

446
00:13:56,927 --> 00:13:58,273
up on a door.

447
00:13:58,313 --> 00:14:01,411
And somebody, somewhere, has to write it.

448
00:14:01,451 --> 00:14:02,766
Twenty rules.

449
00:14:02,806 --> 00:14:04,194
Some of them could save your life this

450
00:14:04,234 --> 00:14:05,533
winter, for real.

451
00:14:05,573 --> 00:14:07,073
Fill the bathtub.

452
00:14:07,113 --> 00:14:08,614
Clear the vents.

453
00:14:08,654 --> 00:14:10,591
Never burn anything indoors.

454
00:14:10,631 --> 00:14:12,785
The rest, well, I hope you never need

455
00:14:12,825 --> 00:14:13,497
them.

456
00:14:13,537 --> 00:14:15,146
But if you ever look out the window during

457
00:14:15,186 --> 00:14:17,143
a storm, and someone's standing there

458
00:14:17,183 --> 00:14:18,861
with no snow on their shoulders,

459
00:14:18,994 --> 00:14:21,001
you already know what to do.

460
00:14:21,041 --> 00:14:22,835
And if you were writing the card, what

461
00:14:22,875 --> 00:14:25,169
would rule twenty-one be?

462
00:14:25,209 --> 00:14:26,914
Tell me in the comments.

463
00:14:26,954 --> 00:14:28,069
I'll be reading them.
